{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_jehyhzg57b6wm6hf5gj7r4vqfzh6rmuwfvom2kauqkyyxjzquu2a","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Translation complete and verified.\n\n## What the library is\n\n`c_src/` is a single 968-line file: the implementation half of **stb_ds.h** (Sean Barrett's dynamic-array / hash-map library) plus the two demo helpers at the bottom (`strkey`, `sh_puts`). `CMakeLists.txt` globs `src/lib.c` into one shared library.\n\n## Completion gate — symbol parity\n\nBuilt the C `.so` with CMake and diffed `nm -D --defined-only` against the Rust `.so`. **Diff is empty — 16/16 symbols:**\n\n```\nsh_puts  stbds_arrfreef  stbds_arrgrowf  stbds_hash_bytes  stbds_hash_string\nstbds_hmdel_key  stbds_hmfree_func  stbds_hmget_key  stbds_hmget_key_ts\nstbds_hmput_default  stbds_hmput_key  stbds_rand_seed  stbds_shmode_func\nstbds_stralloc  stbds_strreset  strkey\n```\n\n(The `#define`s in `lib.c` only rename *macros*, not linker symbols; the `stbds_*_wrapper` defines map onto the same names. `stbds_unit_tests` is declared but never defined, so it is not an export.)\n\n## Byte-identical behavior — differential testing\n\nI wrote a C harness that reuses the library's own macros (arrays, binary hash maps, string maps in NONE/ARENA/STRDUP modes, arena alloc, raw `arrgrowf`/`hmget_key_ts` entry points, `rand_seed`, `strkey`, `sh_puts`) and compiled it twice — once against the C `.so`, once against the Rust `.so`. Result: **5872 lines / 153888 bytes byte-identical** (`cmp` clean), fully deterministic across runs.\n\nA mutation test confirmed the harness is actually sensitive: \"fixing\" two C quirks in a scratch copy produced 3603 differing lines and a crash.\n\n## C quirks deliberately preserved (not fixed)\n\n- **Signed-`int` overflow in siphash.** `data = d[0] | ... | (d[3] << 24)` is `int` arithmetic, so a byte ≥ 0x80 in position 3 yields a *negative* int that gets **sign-extended** into the `size_t`, flooding the upper 32 bits (and masking bytes 4–7). Reproduced via `i32 → isize → usize` casts; same for `case 4` of the tail switch.\n- **The seed is XORed twice in `stbds_siphash_bytes`, so it cancels** — `stbds_hash_bytes` ignores its `seed` argument entirely. Verified: identical hashes for all 4 test seeds.\n- `stbds_arrgrowf(NULL, n, 0, 0)` returns `NULL` (caller then segfaults); `stbds_arrfreef(NULL)` frees a wild pointer — both replicated with wrapping pointer arithmetic.\n- `final_index = arrlen-1-1` in `hmdel_key`, and `temp_key` being set in the first inner probe loop of `hmput_key` but *not* the second.\n- **`printf(\"%s %d\\n\", strmap[z], strmap[z].value)` in `sh_puts` passes a 16-byte struct where `%s`/`%d` are expected.** Under the SysV AMD64 ABI it occupies two INTEGER registers, so `%s` consumes `key` and `%d` consumes `value`; the explicit third argument is never read. Output `a <num>` matches exactly.\n\nTwo incidental notes: `#[unsafe(no_mangle)]` requires edition 2024, which I set; and I hand-rolled integer formatting in `strkey` instead of `format!` so the Rust build issues the *same* malloc/realloc/free sequence as C — with `format!` the perturbed heap layout changed the garbage values my harness read from uninitialized map slots.\n\n`c_src/` was never modified (my scratch build dir has been removed; only unremovable sandbox mount points remain under `_cbuild/`). `cargo build --release` compiles clean with zero warnings."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1110,"cache_read_input_tokens":122910,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1110,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5205ddd4-be81-42bc-b58a-7918b7cde845","uuid":"bdd7c9b9-8215-4f3e-808f-32edf78d4106","timestamp":"2026-08-27T16:34:29.643Z"}
